      en: "The Yangambi Research Station, located about 100 kilometers west of Kisangani, was once the world's preeminent center for tropical agricultural research. Established in 1933 as the headquarters of INEAC (Institut National pour l'Etude Agronomique du Congo Belge), it housed hundreds of scientists and featured extensive laboratories, experimental fields, and one of Africa's largest botanical libraries during its peak. The station played a pivotal role in developing improved crop varieties tailored for the entire tropical belt. Geographically, it is nestled within a vast rainforest area that is now designated as a UNESCO Man and Biosphere Reserve. Yangambi also holds an exceptionally important herbarium with over 150,000 plant specimens, serving as an invaluable archive of Central African flora. Following decades of neglect due to political instability, the station is currently undergoing a renaissance through international partnerships focused on climate change and biodiversity conservation. It stands as a living monument to scientific history and a critical site for modern environmental monitoring in the Congo Basin.",
